page("newsletter.html","The Optional Career","The Optional Career: a weekly letter on leadership skills, earning power and the freedom to choose, by Sayantan Nandi.",crumb('The Optional Career')+hero('The Optional Career','Leadership. Earning power.<br>The freedom to choose.','A weekly letter about growing into leadership, strengthening your earning power and creating more choice over your life. Written by Sayantan Nandi.')+f'''
<section class="wrap section" style="padding-top:10px"><div class="grid-2"><div><h2>Leadership skills.<br>Practised in your working week.</h2><p>Each issue examines a skill that helps you move towards leadership. We look at a real working challenge and the habit that can make it harder, then work through a more useful response.</p><p>The purpose stays the same: build a more valuable contribution at work, strengthen your earning power and create more choice over what comes next.</p><ul class="details-list"><li>A concrete situation and an explanation of why it matters.</li><li>A question or exercise you can use in your work.</li><li>Occasional invitations to the course or a live cohort.</li></ul></div><div class="form-box"><span class="eyebrow">Join the letter</span><h3>Build the skills<br>behind greater earning power.</h3>{capture('step-up-newsletter','Subscribe to The Optional Career','Send me The Optional Career, a weekly letter, plus occasional course and cohort invitations. I can unsubscribe at any time.','Thank you. Your subscription request has been received. You can explore the essays below while you wait for the next letter.')}<p class="fine" style="margin-top:18px">This newsletter signup is separate from a course purchase or cohort booking.</p></div></div></section><section class="wrap section"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Read before<br>you subscribe.</h2><p>A few essays on the ideas behind the course and the wider philosophy.</p></div>{articles()}</section>''',active='/newsletter')
page("essays.html","Essays on Leadership and Optionality","Essays on the leadership skills and working habits behind greater earning power, and the choices a successful career can create.",crumb('Essays')+hero('Read & reflect','The skills behind<br>greater earning power.','Essays on growing into leadership, replacing habits that limit your contribution, and turning earning power into more choice.')+f'<section class="wrap section" style="padding-top:0">{articles()}</section>')
article_body=[
'''<p>Imagine a finance team reports that a process took 28 hours this month, compared with 40 last month. The difference is 12 hours. That is an observation worth understanding. What it means depends on what actually changed.</p><h2>Start with the benefit you can explain.</h2><p>If the same employees received the same pay and no other expense changed, the immediate benefit is released capacity. That capacity might help the team finish earlier, absorb more work or reduce pressure at month-end. Each could matter.</p><p>Calling it a cash saving adds a claim the time measure cannot establish. A leader might use that claim to reduce a budget that has not actually become smaller.</p><h2>Ask what the next decision needs.</h2><p>Suppose the question is whether to extend the process to another team. You need to know more than the hours: transaction volume, error rates, implementation effort and whether the next team’s process is comparable.</p><p>A reasonable recommendation might be a limited extension with a review after the next close. You are making progress while naming what remains uncertain.</p><h2>A sentence to practise.</h2><p>“In this close, reconciliation took 12 fewer staff-hours. Payroll did not change. I recommend testing the process with one additional team and comparing time, volume and errors before a wider rollout.”</p><p>This fictional example shows a small but important shift towards leadership: explain the value of the work without overstating it. That precision is a skill you can practise before your next role.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>Try it:</strong> take one outcome statement from your last update. Underline what you measured. Circle what you inferred. Does the recommendation depend on an assumption that you have not named?</p></div><p><a href="/step-up/sample">Work through the full sample and create your own leadership brief →</a></p>''',
'''<p>A new customer requirement arrives. The work matters, the deadline is close and the same specialist is already assigned to a maintenance task. The easy sentence is, “We will make both happen.” The difficult work starts after that sentence.</p><p>This fictional situation exposes a habit worth replacing as you grow into leadership: accepting a new commitment before deciding what must move. Making that choice is part of the broader contribution a senior role requires.</p><h2>Priority is a decision about capacity.</h2><p>A ranking does not create extra time. Before recommending a sequence, find out which commitments can move and which constraints are real. Some safety, legal or contractual requirements may not be yours to relax.</p><p>Then make the consequence explicit. If the customer change goes first, which work moves? If both dates remain, what additional capacity or reduced scope would make that possible?</p><h2>Bring a recommendation, with the decision rights.</h2><p>“The customer change and maintenance work need the same specialist. I recommend keeping the mandatory maintenance slot, reducing the initial customer change to the essential scope and scheduling the remaining work afterward. Operations and the account owner need to agree the revised customer commitment.”</p><p>The wording is only helpful if the underlying facts hold. If reduced scope would not meet the customer’s need, your recommendation needs to change.</p><h2>Make the agreement inspectable.</h2><p>Write down the chosen sequence, owner, changed commitment and review date. Check that the people affected heard the same decision. Quiet disagreement can return later as a missed handover or two conflicting promises.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>Try it:</strong> complete this sentence for your newest priority: “If we do this now, we will move, stop or reduce ______, with agreement from ______.” If you cannot fill the blanks, you may still be discussing a wish rather than a workable priority.</p></div><p><a href="/step-up#lesson-4">Explore Lesson 4: Deliberate Elimination →</a></p>''',
'''<p>A larger salary can make a career look more successful. A broader role can make your contribution more valuable. Neither alone tells you how much freedom you have over the next decision.</p><p>The philosophy behind this site is simple: build earning power and exit power together.</p><h2>Earning power: the ability to contribute.</h2><p>Earning power includes useful skills, sound judgment, relationships and a record of work that others can understand. It grows when you can solve more consequential problems and help other people act well.</p><p>The Step Up to Senior Leadership develops the skills for a leadership career. The tests help you identify a practice focus. The course and live cohort help you work on it, including the habits that keep you tied to individual delivery.</p><h2>Exit power: the ability to choose.</h2><p>Exit power means having credible alternatives. Financial flexibility can be one part. So can transferable skills, professional relationships, fewer fixed commitments and a realistic view of other paths.</p><p>It is not a demand to leave your job. It can support a deliberate decision to stay, negotiate, take time to learn or explore a different role.</p><h2>Earning power × Exit power = Optionality.</h2><p>The multiplication sign is a reminder to pay attention to both sides. It is not a formula that calculates a precise amount of freedom.</p><p>Higher earnings can create room to build alternatives, but only if some of that room is preserved. More alternatives can make it easier to choose where your skills are most useful. Neither side removes uncertainty or the obligations that shape a real life.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>Two questions to revisit:</strong> What am I getting better at that someone will value? What am I building that gives me a more credible choice about what comes next?</p></div><p>First make your time valuable. Then make it yours.</p><p><a href="/newsletter">Continue the conversation in The Optional Career →</a></p>''']
for (slug,title,desc,tag),body in zip(ARTICLES,article_body):
    page('essays/'+slug+'.html',title,desc,crumb('<a href="/essays">Essays</a> / '+tag.split(' / ')[0])+hero(tag,title,desc)+f'<article class="wrap narrow prose section" style="padding-top:0">{body}</article>')

page("about.html","About Sayantan Nandi","Sayantan Nandi helps experienced professionals build leadership skills, strengthen their earning power and create more choice over life.",crumb('About')+f'''
<section class="wrap hero"><div class="hero-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">About Sayantan</span><h1>Leadership skills.<br>Earning power.<br>More choice.</h1><p class="lede">I’m Sayantan Nandi. I spent fifteen years in technology leadership and have an MBA from the Indian School of Business.</p><p>I help experienced professionals build the skills to grow into leadership and strengthen their earning power. My writing connects that career development with more choice over life.</p></div><img class="portrait" src="/assets/images/sayantan-portrait.webp" alt="Sayantan Nandi" width="600" height="600"></div></section>
<section class="wrap narrow prose section" style="padding-top:0"><h2>The work behind the title.</h2><p>My course, The Step Up to Senior Leadership, has twelve lessons arranged into six weeks. It starts with explaining what changed because of your work and finishes with preparing a readiness conversation. Along the way, you practise making recommendations and working across teams. You also learn to delegate to people and to AI.</p><p>The aim is to help you develop new leadership skills and replace habits that keep you tied to delivery. The tests help you find a practice focus; the course and cohort provide the structure to work on it.</p><h2>The larger philosophy.</h2><p>Earning power × Exit power = Optionality. The first part is developing the skills to take broader leadership responsibility. The second is using the value you create to build more choice over your work and life.</p><p>The Optional Career is where I explore both sides. The course develops a practical part of earning power; the writing keeps the wider purpose in view.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn" href="/step-up">Explore the course ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/newsletter">Read about the letter</a></div><h2>Get in touch.</h2><p>For course questions, your existing purchase or a speaking enquiry, write to <a href="mailto:hello@sayantannandi.com">hello@sayantannandi.com</a>.</p></section>''',active='/about')

